INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261126-fcc-abc-licenses-injunction', 'judge-alikhan-blocks-fcc-early-license-review-abc-stations-first-amendment-retaliation', 'newsroom', 'news_story', 'courts', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'Judge blocks the FCC''s early license review of ABC''s eight stations, finding likely retaliation', 'U.S. District Judge Loren AliKhan issued a preliminary injunction on Wednesday, Nov. 25, and wrote that the case was not a close one, CBS News and UPI reported.', 'A federal judge on Wednesday, Nov. 25, blocked the Federal Communications Commission from moving ahead with an early license review of eight ABC-owned television stations, CBS News and UPI reported.

U.S. District Judge Loren AliKhan granted a preliminary injunction in a 45-page ruling that bars the FCC from further action against ABC in the early renewal proceedings while the lawsuit continues, according to CBS News. She also denied the FCC''s motion to dismiss the case, UPI reported.

## What the FCC did

Earlier this year the FCC ordered the eight stations to file for license renewal early, citing possible violations of the Communications Act of 1934 and its ban on unlawful discrimination, CBS News reported. The licenses were originally due for renewal between 2028 and 2031. UPI reported that the order cited an investigation into Disney and ABC employment practices, including diversity policies the administration calls discriminatory.

Disney, ABC and the stations sued in August, arguing that the move was retaliation for coverage critical of Trump, in violation of the First Amendment, according to UPI.

## The judge''s reasoning

AliKhan found ABC was likely to succeed in showing retaliation, CBS News reported. The network said it documented at least 11 occasions on which Trump urged the FCC to strip its licenses, and the judge wrote that Chairman Brendan Carr had taken the president''s rhetoric as his marching orders, according to CBS News. She declined a broader request to bar any informal pressure on the company.

## Reactions

ABC News called the decision an important victory for the First Amendment, CBS News reported. An FCC spokesperson said the ruling was wrong on the facts and the law and that the agency expects to prevail. Commissioner Anna Gomez, the only Democrat on the five-member commission, welcomed the ruling, UPI reported.', 'Boston Daily Staff', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/FCC_HQ.jpg/1280px-FCC_HQ.jpg', 'Federal Communications Commission headquarters in Washington (file photo). Via Wikimedia Commons; editor to confirm the photographer and license on the file page', 'licensed', 'newsroom', 'content/2026-11-26.sql', 8, 0, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-11-26T11:00:00.000Z', '2026-11-26T11:00:00.000Z');
